<p>Whether you&rsquo;re a new user or a seasoned pro, ScreenCastsONLINE offers in-depth screencasts on a wide range of topics, from tutorials to app discovery. <a href="https://screencastsonline.com/members/aff/go/bterpstra">Check it out.</a></p>

<dl>
  <dt><a href="https://apps.shakingthehabitual.com/traverse/">Traverse</a></dt>
  <dd>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Traverse is a macOS outliner with a focused Writer view, flexible outlines, column browsing, attributes, filtering and portable OPML files.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </dd>
  <dd>
    <p>I really love this app. It&rsquo;s a powerful horizontal outliner (I was a huge Tree fan back in the day) with a plain text (OPML) file format and a very complete feature set. I don&rsquo;t think it&rsquo;s as scriptable as Bike, but the GUI is very complete.</p>
  </dd>
  <dd>
    <p>Plus, it can stream to Marked 3!</p>
  </dd>
  <dt><a href="https://klipto.me/">Klipto</a></dt>
  <dd>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Klipto is a fast, native clipboard manager for macOS: hold-to-paste, Stack, smart transforms and OCR search. A one-time, local, no-subscription alternative to Paste, Maccy, Pastebot and Clipy.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </dd>
  <dd>
    <p>This is a cool take on clipboard management. The form filling capability is really nice and it&rsquo;s less complicated (to me) than the Keyboard Maestro clipboard setup.</p>
  </dd>
  <dt><a href="https://github.com/rhsev/fileregister">rhsev/fileregister</a></dt>
  <dd>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Permanent ids and self-documenting binders for your files, in plain text; references survive renames, moves, and a change of machine.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </dd>
  <dd>
    <p>This is a very cool one from Ralf Hülsmann.</p>
  </dd>
  <dt><a href="https://luckydangle.app/">Lucky Dangle</a></dt>
  <dd>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Choose a lucky charm and hang it from the top of your Mac. It sways while you work, stays out of every click, and drops in when you call it.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </dd>
  <dd>
    <p>Ok, the developer pitched this and I said &ldquo;why?&rdquo;. Then I read <a href="https://karthikmahadevan.com/lucky-dangle">this blog post</a> and I&rsquo;m sold on it as entertainment, with various types of spiritual significance from a range of cultures. Plus, the physics are really cool.</p>
  </dd>
  <dt><a href="https://tidymacapp.com/">Tidy — your Mac tidies itself</a></dt>
  <dd>
    <blockquote>
      <p>Screenshots archived, installers ejected, Downloads sorted — all on its own, from your menu bar. $9 once, no subscription. Works with Apple Intelligence.</p>
    </blockquote>
  </dd>
  <dd>
    <p>This is another entry in what&rsquo;s getting to be a crowded field, but in testing it, it seemed to be one of the more solid options, and I especially like the auto-ejecting and trashing of old DMG files. Making images searchable by content is a nice touch, even if not entirely novel.</p>
  </dd>
  <dd>
    <p>$9 one time, macOS 14+, Intel and Apple Silicon, no account or telemetry, and the OCR and content search work everywhere — only the natural-language rules need macOS 26.</p>
  </dd>
</dl>

